    "## 4. What proportion of patients experienced readmission and mortality within 28 days, 3 months, and 6 months of discharge?\n",
    "### Reasoning:  \n",
    "\n",
    "Hospital readmissions and post-discharge mortality are key quality-of-care metrics.\n",
    "\n",
    "Patients  28 days,3 months, and 6 months readmission rates are standard quality metrics hospitals report (e.g., CMS 30-day readmission measures use a similar window), so these numbers let you compare against national or peer benchmarks.Tracking these three windows over time or across departments highlights whether early (28-day, often \"preventable\") readmissions vs. later ones (3-6 month, often disease-progression driven) are the bigger problem.\n",
    "\n",
    "Reporting mortality across the same three windows lets us compare this cohort against national or peer benchmarks using a consistent, internationally recognized time frame, rather than an arbitrary cutoff\n",
    "\n",
    "### Metrics used:\n",
    "The raw dataset gives us two ways to measure them:\n",
    "\n",
    "<h6>Flag columns</h6> (readmission_28d, readmission_3m, readmission_6m, death_28d, etc.) — pre-computed binary indicators\n",
    "<h6>Time-to-event columns</h6> (readmission_time_days, death_time_days) — the actual number of days until the event occurred\n",
    "\n",
    "We used second method to measure readmission rate and mortality rate for accuracy instead of flag columns.\n",
    "\n",
    "admission_date – shows when the patient was admitted\n",
    "\n",
    "readmission_time_days -the actual number of days to readmit hospital\n",
    "\n",
    "death_time_days -the actual number of days until the death\n"
   ]
  },
